course: adult-life
lesson: 37
title: Sorry, white lies and «because I said so»
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Grown-ups have a set of everyday phrases that do not always mean exactly what the words say: "We'll see", "Because I said so", "We must do this again soon".
explain: Some are polite habits, some are small kind untruths called white lies, and some are real, important feelings like sorry and proud.
explain: Vivid example: at Christmas in Dublin, Auntie Siobhan hands Mum a lumpy orange jumper. Mum beams, "Oh, I love it!" On the drive home she laughs, "It's awful, but she knitted it for weeks, God love her."
explain: Learning to hear what a phrase really means helps you know when to wait, when to ask again later, and when a grown-up is telling you something that truly matters.
fact: When a grown-up says "I'm sorry, I was out of order" after a row, it is a real apology to another adult; adults argue and make up too.
fact: A real apology names what you did wrong and does not add "but".
fact: Seeing parents make up after an argument is a good thing: people can disagree and still love each other.
fact: A white lie is a small untruth told to protect someone's feelings, like "I love it!" about a present you don't like; it thanks the person for their kindness.
fact: Most people think tiny kind lies are OK, but big lies that hurt people or hide important things are not.
fact: "Thank you, that's so kind" is a way to be honest and kind at the same time.
fact: "We must do this again soon!" is a polite phrase to end a visit nicely; it is not a firm plan.
fact: "Because I said so" usually means the parent is too tired to explain again, or the decision is about safety or fairness and is made; a calm "Can you tell me why?" later works better than arguing.
fact: A parent showing your drawing to everyone at work is proud of you; parents show off their children because it makes them happy.
fact: "We'll see" often means "probably not, but I don't want to say no now", and sometimes it really means "I need to think".
fact: If a "We'll see" matters to you, ask calmly what would need to change for it to become a yes.
fact: "Mustn't grumble" is an old-fashioned way of saying "I'd rather not complain"; many older people hide small pains so others won't worry.
fact: Asking kindly, "Does it hurt?" gives an older person a chance to be honest.
fact: Parents feel nervous for the people they love even when only watching, then pride and relief rush in; their nerves show how much they care, not that they thought you'd fail.
exercise: After a row with Mum, Dad says, "I'm sorry, I was out of order." What is he doing? | Giving Mum a real apology | Telling her the car is broken | Ordering a takeaway for them | Blaming Mum for the row
exercise: What makes an apology a real one? | It names what you did wrong and adds no "but" | It is said very loudly so everyone hears | It comes with a present every time | It explains why the other person was worse
exercise: Your parents argued but made up the same evening. What does that show? | People can disagree and still love each other | They will separate very soon | Arguing is never allowed in proper families, so someone broke a rule | One of them was lying about the row
exercise: Mum opens Auntie's jumper and says, "Oh, I love it!" though she doesn't. What is that? | A white lie to protect Auntie's feelings | A big lie that will hurt Auntie when she finds out | A joke Auntie is meant to laugh at | A promise to wear it every day
exercise: Which kind of lie do most people think is NOT OK? | A big lie that hurts people or hides something important | Saying you like a present to be kind | Saying a meal was lovely when it was only fine and a bit too salty | Saying "nice haircut" to cheer a friend up
exercise: Which answer to a present you don't like is honest AND kind? | "Thank you, that's so kind of you" | "I hate it, take it back" | "This is the best thing I've ever owned" | Saying nothing and walking off
exercise: Waving goodbye, Dad calls to the neighbours, "We must do this again soon!" What does it usually mean? | "That was nice", without a firm plan | They are coming back next Saturday | He wants them to leave faster | He has booked a party already
exercise: You ask "why?" for the fifth time and Mum says, "Because I said so." What is most likely true? | She is tired of explaining, or the decision is made | She doesn't know the answer at all | She wants you to keep asking why | She is only joking and will change her mind in a minute
exercise: What is the best thing to do after "Because I said so"? | Later, calmly ask "Can you tell me why?" | Argue louder until she gives in | Do the thing anyway behind her back | Never speak to her again about it
exercise: Dad is grinning and showing your drawing to everyone at work. Why? | He is proud of you | He wants to make fun of you | He needs a picture for the office wall | His boss asked him to bring art in
exercise: You ask for a puppy and Mum says, "We'll see." What does that often mean? | Probably not, but she doesn't want to say no now | Yes, the puppy is already ordered and arrives next week | Definitely no, and never ask again | She wants to see a photo of the puppy
exercise: If a "We'll see" really matters to you, what's the smart move? | Calmly ask what would need to change for a yes | Ask every five minutes until she says yes | Sulk until she feels sorry for you | Get the puppy from a friend yourself
exercise: Grandad's knees clearly hurt, but he says, "Mustn't grumble." What does he mean? | He'd rather not complain or make a fuss | His knees feel perfectly fine and never hurt him | He is cross with you for asking | He wants a new pair of knees
exercise: What can you do when an older person hides that something hurts? | Kindly ask, "Does it hurt?" | Tell them to stop moaning | Pretend you didn't notice at all | Tell everyone at school about it
exercise: After the school play, Mum says, "You were brilliant, and I was so nervous for you!" Why was she nervous? | Grown-ups feel nervous for people they love | She thought you would surely fail and forget your lines | She was worried about her own costume | She had to go on stage after you
test: Your sister says, "Mum lied about Auntie's jumper, so Mum's a liar like a thief." Put her right. | It was a small kind white lie, not a harmful one | She's right, it's just as bad as stealing from the shop | It wasn't a lie, Mum really loves orange | Mum was only lying to get another jumper
test: Mum and Dad shouted at each other on Tuesday; by Wednesday Dad says, "I was bang out of order, love, sorry." What happened? | They argued and then made up | They are still angry and won't talk | Dad is apologising to the children | Mum is the one who said sorry
test: Overheard: "I'm sorry, BUT if you hadn't nagged me..." Why is this a weak apology? | The "but" blames the other person | It is too short to count | It doesn't use the word "sorry" | It was said in a quiet voice
test: Neighbour, leaving: "Lovely evening! We must get together again soon!" Your little brother wants to pack for their house tomorrow. Put him right. | It's just a polite goodbye, not a real plan | Yes, pack now, they invited us | No, it means they never want to see us | Yes, but only if it's sunny
test: Dad, worn out: "No more questions. Because I said so, OK?" What will probably work better than arguing? | Asking calmly later why, so you understand | Asking the same question twenty more times | Going to ask Grandad behind Dad's back | Slamming the door to make your point
test: Dad's colleague texts: "Your Ella's dinosaur pic is on the fridge here, you never shut up about her lol." How does Dad feel? | Proud of Ella | Embarrassed by Ella | Annoyed at Ella | Worried about Ella
test: Your mate says: "my mum said 'we'll see' about a PS5, so that's a yes!" Is that right? | Not really, "we'll see" often means probably not | Yes, "we'll see" always means yes, just not straight away | Yes, it means it's being bought today | No, it means she's angry with him
test: Nan, limping: "Oh, mustn't grumble, love, put the kettle on." What might you kindly ask? | "Does your leg hurt, Nan?" | "Why are you always complaining?" | "Can I have a biscuit too?" | "Why are you walking funny?"
test: Mum after your football final: "God, I was a wreck watching you, but you were AMAZING." What do her nerves show? | How much she cares about you | That she expected you to lose | That she hates football matches | That she wanted to play instead
test: Auntie gives you socks with ducks on. Which answer is kind without being a big lie? | "Thank you, that's so kind of you!" | "Ugh, ducks are for babies" | "These are the best socks in the world, ever!" | "I'll give them to the dog"
test: Your friend lies to the teacher that another child broke the window, to get out of trouble. What kind of lie is that? | A harmful lie that is not OK | A kind white lie that is fine | A polite phrase everyone understands | A good secret about a surprise
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "Oh, I love it!" uttered by a recipient who does not, in fact, care for the gift received. | A white lie intended to protect the feelings of the giver | A serious deception intended to cause harm | A firm undertaking to use the gift daily | An expression of sincere and entirely unqualified admiration for it
exam: Identify which of the following constitutes a genuine apology in the sense established in this lesson. | A statement naming the wrongdoing without qualification | A statement of regret followed by the word "but" and a complaint | A statement attributing the fault to the injured party | A statement offering a gift in place of acknowledgement
exam: It is given that a parent responds to a request for a puppy with the words "We'll see". Determine the most probable meaning of this response. | Probably not, although no refusal is yet stated | An unconditional promise that a puppy will be obtained | A request to inspect a photograph of the animal | A final and permanent prohibition of the subject
exam: Whereas an elderly relative with evident pain in the knees remarks "Mustn't grumble", ascertain the meaning of the remark. | He prefers not to complain about his discomfort | He reports that he is entirely free of pain | He reproaches the listener for complaining | He requests immediate medical attention
